<div class="head blk kn" data-id="sunday-lead"><div class="kicker">The big read · AI and money</div><h3>Inside Anthropic's S-1: a company that grew 14 times in a year, "lost" 80 billion, and will not be controlled by its shareholders</h3>
<template class="more"><h5>Background</h5><p>Anthropic submitted a confidential draft in June, valued at 965 billion dollars by private investors in May, and made the filing public on Monday 26 October. The dailies reported the headline (a Nasdaq listing as ANT). This read covers the mechanics: what the numbers mean, who holds the votes, and the dates that decide whether you can buy it.</p><h5>Key facts</h5><ul><li>Revenue: 41 million dollars (2023), 386 million (2024), 4.6 billion (2025), 16.3 billion in the first half of 2026.</li><li>Cash and short-term investments: about 131.7 billion dollars on 30 June 2026, after 141.1 billion raised since 2021.</li><li>Paying customers on 30 September: over 11,900 above 100,000 dollars a year, over 1,900 above 1 million, about 250 above 10 million.</li><li>Staff: about 5,300, so revenue per employee above 6.6 million dollars over the past year.</li><li>Class F share (seven co-founders): 50.1% of votes on most matters. Class T share (Long-Term Benefit Trust): elects a board majority.</li></ul><h5>What happens next</h5><p>Still blank in the draft: share count, price range and third-quarter results. An amended filing with a range starts the roadshow. Reports (not the filing) point to pricing in mid or late November; nothing is fixed until the underwriters price it. Lock-ups release in more than 20 windows over about a year, with some expiring on days 27, 48, 62 and 76 after listing.</p><figure class="photo blk" data-wiki="en|Nasdaq" data-alt="Nasdaq MarketSite in Times Square"><figcaption><b>File photo.</b> The Nasdaq MarketSite tower in Times Square, New York, where ANT is meant to trade.</figcaption></figure><div class="srcs"><a href="https://www.commonsllc.com/insights/anthropic-ipo-s1-filing-plain-english">Commons Capital: Anthropic's IPO Filing, in Plain English</a> <a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/live/anthropic-ipo-live-updates-frontier-ai-lab-discloses-revenue-and-risks-in-highly-anticipated-s-1-filing-141702607.html">Yahoo Finance: Anthropic IPO live updates</a> <a href="https://www.clickondetroit.com/business/2026/10/26/artificial-intelligence-giant-anthropic-files-papers-to-sell-shares-to-the-public-for-the-first-time/">AP via WDIV: Anthropic files papers to sell shares</a></div></template></div>
<div class="inbrief blk"><b class="l">In brief</b>Anthropic's public S-1 shows 16.3 billion dollars of revenue in six months and an 80.7 billion net loss, of which 72.4 billion is a non-cash revaluation charge. The operating line is near break-even. Buyers get Class A shares with less than half the votes. Pricing is reported for the second half of November.</div>
<figure class="photo blk" data-src="https://bc.gmg.io/image/upload/q_auto,d_https%3A%3A%3Amedia.clickondetroit.com%3Atheme%3Aimages%3Aplaceholder-16x9-wdiv.png,c_lfill,w_2560,h_1707,f_webp/media/gmg/NCK46TJCRZDG7NAUCZZKG2ND5A.jpg?api_key=clickondetroit_06f87fefb2123598" data-credit="AP Photo/Markus Schreiber" data-link="https://www.clickondetroit.com/business/2026/10/26/artificial-intelligence-giant-anthropic-files-papers-to-sell-shares-to-the-public-for-the-first-time/" data-alt="Dario Amodei at Davos"><figcaption><b>File photo, Davos, 23 January 2025.</b> Anthropic chief executive and co-founder Dario Amodei at the World Economic Forum. Not taken on the filing day.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="blk drop">Start with the loss, because it is the number everyone repeats and the one that means least. Of the 80.7 billion dollars lost in the first half of 2026, about 72.4 billion is an accounting charge: instruments such as convertible notes were revalued upward as the company's own valuation rose, and no cash left the building. Strip that out and the operating loss for the half was 693 million dollars, against 8.1 billion for all of 2025. The second quarter alone showed a first operating profit, 974 million dollars, after a 1.7 billion loss in the first.</p>
<p class="blk">The revenue curve explains the swing. Second-quarter revenue was 11.6 billion dollars against 4.7 billion in the first, while compute costs rose from 3.4 to 5.6 billion. Revenue grew far faster than the biggest cost line. The filing credits products such as Claude Code and Claude Cowork for demand, and counts roughly 250 customers paying over 10 million dollars a year. Treat adjusted figures (1.6 billion dollars of non-GAAP operating income) as management's view: they leave out items the company chooses.</p>
<p class="blk">The risk is the other side of the same coin: compute. Anthropic has bought chips and data-centre capacity years ahead from a handful of suppliers, some of which are also investors, customers or competitors. Commitments run as far as 2045. One deal alone commits up to 120.5 billion dollars through 2029 for Nvidia GPUs via SpaceXAI, about 3.1 billion a month once all capacity is delivered, expected in December. Press reports put the wider spending plan at 542 billion dollars or more; the dollar total is blank in the draft itself, so treat that as a reported estimate.</p>
<div class="box blk"><h4>Who controls ANT</h4><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Holder</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Class A</td><td>Public buyers</td><td>One vote per share; together under half the vote</td></tr><tr><td>Class F</td><td>Seven co-founders (via an LLC)</td><td>50.1% of votes on most matters, including the remaining directors</td></tr><tr><td>Class T</td><td>Long-Term Benefit Trust, five unconflicted trustees</td><td>Elects a majority of the board</td></tr></table></div></div>
<p class="blk">That table is the part a fund manager will price. Anthropic is a Delaware public benefit corporation, so the board must weigh shareholders' financial interests against a stated purpose, the responsible development of advanced AI for the long-term benefit of humanity. It will be a "controlled company" under Nasdaq rules. Buyers share the economics but not the control.</p>
<p class="blk">Two dilution terms matter. Five percent of fully diluted shares are set aside for a wholly owned public benefit fund, and the board approved a framework to issue up to 2% more shares each time the price rises 50% above the previous milestone, starting at the IPO price. The co-founders have pledged 80% of their personal equity to philanthropy. Read those as a signal about who the company thinks its owners are.</p>
<p class="blk">The filing also lists risks that read oddly beside a share offering: models that could act in unintended ways, insurance that may not cover a catastrophic AI incident, and a dispute with the US government. The Trump administration labelled Anthropic a supply chain risk in February after it refused unrestricted military use of its models; it sued the Pentagon in March, won in one court and lost in another, and both sides are appealing.</p>
<ol class="tl blk"><li><b>Feb 2026</b> US administration designates Anthropic a supply chain risk.</li><li><b>Mar 2026</b> Anthropic sues the Pentagon.</li><li><b>May 2026</b> Private valuation: 965 billion dollars.</li><li><b>Jun 2026</b> Confidential draft S-1 submitted.</li><li><b>26 Oct</b> Public S-1: Class A on Nasdaq as ANT, Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley leading 20-plus banks.</li><li><b>Reported mid or late Nov</b> Pricing and first trade; valuation talk of 1.5 to 2 trillion dollars.</li><li><b>Dec</b> Full SpaceXAI GPU capacity due.</li><li><b>Early 2027</b> OpenAI reportedly delays its own IPO to this window.</li></ol>
<p class="blk">Scenarios. If it prices in the reported 1.5 to 2 trillion dollar range and raises more than SpaceX's 86 billion in June, it becomes the largest IPO on record (AP calls it potentially the biggest ever; the raise is unconfirmed, with reports from 60 billion to over 100 billion). If markets stay as shaky as on Friday, when Intel fell hardest since 1974 and the Nasdaq dropped 2.9%, expect a delay or a lower range, not a cancelled deal. The tell is the amended S-1 with a price range.</p>
<div class="you blk"><b>Why it matters to you.</b> You cannot buy it on day one through your Revolut plan, but 30.6% of your ETFs sit in EQQB (Nasdaq 100, target 15%). A giant ANT listing will enter the Nasdaq 100 only after the index rules allow it, and it drains cash from the same big-tech names you already hold. Your rule is to add money to the underweight lines (SEC0, CEBL), not to chase an IPO pop.</div>

<div class="head blk kn" data-id="sunday-profile"><div class="kicker">Profile · Formula 1</div><h3>Kimi Antonelli, 20, one race from being champion</h3>
<template class="more"><h5>Background</h5><p>Andrea Kimi Antonelli was born on 25 August 2006 in Bologna. His father Marco is a former racing driver who runs Antonelli Motorsport, the team he karted for from age seven.</p><h5>Key facts</h5><ul><li>2019: joined the Mercedes Junior Programme.</li><li>2022: won both Italian F4 and ADAC F4, with 22 wins from 35 starts across the two series.</li><li>2023: Formula Regional Middle East title, then the FRECA title, clinched at Zandvoort.</li><li>2024: Formula 2 with Prema, youngest multiple winner in F2 history.</li><li>2025: F1 debut at Melbourne for Mercedes, P4 from P16; highest-scoring rookie in F1 history.</li><li>2026: youngest Grand Prix polesitter and second-youngest winner (Shanghai), first Italian winner since Fisichella in 2006.</li></ul><h5>What happens next</h5><p>He leads Russell by 123 points with five rounds left. Tonight's Mexico City Grand Prix (20:00 Amsterdam time) could give him the title with four races to spare.</p><div class="srcs"><a href="https://mercedesamgf1.com/news/kimi-antonelli-everything-you-need-to-know">Mercedes-AMG F1: Kimi Antonelli, everything you need to know</a></div></template></div>
<figure class="photo blk portrait" data-src="https://images.ctfassets.net/1fvlg6xqnm65/1Oo9ltIQTQNVSshYdXTaMB/7b8399457cb4c24682fc7197ff90399f/KIMI-EYTNK-NEW-2026-MAIN.jpg" data-credit="Mercedes-AMG Petronas F1 Team" data-link="https://mercedesamgf1.com/news/kimi-antonelli-everything-you-need-to-know" data-alt="Kimi Antonelli"><figcaption><b>Kimi Antonelli.</b> Official team portrait from the Mercedes-AMG F1 profile page; undated.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="blk drop">The pole in Mexico belonged to Hamilton, but the story of the weekend is the 20-year-old in fourth. Antonelli arrived in F1 as a project: Mercedes signed him at 12 and built a path through karting, Formula 4, Formula Regional and a single season of Formula 2 before promoting him straight into Hamilton's old seat for 2025.</p>
<p class="blk">The records list is long because he has been the youngest at almost every step: youngest to lead a lap, youngest to set a fastest lap, youngest pole in any F1 format (the Miami Sprint), and in 2026 the youngest Grand Prix polesitter and second-youngest winner, behind Verstappen, at Shanghai. The Mercedes profile also notes he is the first Italian to win a Grand Prix since 2006.</p>
<p class="blk">What the numbers say about tonight: 365 points to Russell's 242 with 125 left. If Hamilton wins he needs tenth or better; if Russell wins, ninth or better; any other winner and he is champion regardless. The risk is not pace but the first lap, with one of the longest runs to turn one on the calendar and Verstappen starting fifth. The result was not known when this paper closed.</p>

<div class="box blk"><h4>Rigging masterclass: a stretchy spline-IK spine with advanced twist and volume</h4>
<p><b>The problem.</b> A spine or tail on a spline IK looks fine until the animator pulls the controls apart: the joints do not stretch, the twist between hips and chest drifts, and any stretch makes the body look thinner instead of fatter. Three separate fixes, built once and reused.</p>
<p><b>The plan.</b> (1) Spline IK with Advanced Twist Controls fed by the hip and chest control matrices, so twist interpolates along the chain with no extra joints. (2) Stretch from the curve's arc length: a curveInfo gives the current length, a divide gives the scale factor. (3) Volume: the factor raised to the power of minus one half drives the two cross-section axes, so the joint gets fatter as it gets longer.</p>
<pre>import maya.cmds as cmds
jnts = ['spine_%02d_jnt' % i for i in range(1, 6)]   # aim axis X
hip, chest = 'hip_ctl', 'chest_ctl'
ik, eff, crv = cmds.ikHandle(sj=jnts[0], ee=jnts[-1], sol='ikSplineSolver',
                             ccv=True, pcv=False, ns=2, n='spine_ik')
crv = cmds.rename(crv, 'spine_crv')
cmds.setAttr(ik + '.dTwistControlEnable', 1)
cmds.setAttr(ik + '.dWorldUpType', 4)        # object rotation up, start/end
cmds.setAttr(ik + '.dForwardAxis', 0)        # +X along the bones
cmds.setAttr(ik + '.dWorldUpAxis', 0)        # +Y
for a in ('dWorldUpVector', 'dWorldUpVectorEnd'):
    cmds.setAttr(ik + '.' + a, 0, 1, 0, type='double3')
cmds.connectAttr(hip + '.worldMatrix[0]', ik + '.dWorldUpMatrix')
cmds.connectAttr(chest + '.worldMatrix[0]', ik + '.dWorldUpMatrixEnd')
info = cmds.createNode('curveInfo', n='spine_info')
cmds.connectAttr(crv + '.worldSpace[0]', info + '.inputCurve')
rest = cmds.getAttr(info + '.arcLength')
fac = cmds.createNode('multiplyDivide', n='spine_stretch_md')
cmds.setAttr(fac + '.operation', 2)          # divide
cmds.setAttr(fac + '.input2X', rest)
cmds.connectAttr(info + '.arcLength', fac + '.input1X')
vol = cmds.createNode('multiplyDivide', n='spine_volume_md')
cmds.setAttr(vol + '.operation', 3)          # power
cmds.setAttr(vol + '.input2X', -0.5)
cmds.connectAttr(fac + '.outputX', vol + '.input1X')
for j in jnts:
    cmds.connectAttr(fac + '.outputX', j + '.scaleX')
    cmds.connectAttr(vol + '.outputX', j + '.scaleY')
    cmds.connectAttr(vol + '.outputX', j + '.scaleZ')</pre>
<p><b>Check.</b> Skin the curve to hip, mid and chest joints (<code>cmds.skinCluster</code> on 3 to 4 joints), then pull the chest control up 30 cm: joints should lengthen and thicken, and rotating the chest 90 degrees about its length should spread the twist evenly. If the spine flips, change dWorldUpAxis to match the axis your controls point up. Set the joints' Segment Scale Compensate off so the scale does not compound down the chain, and clamp the factor with a clamp node if animators over-stretch. Enum values and attribute names are from the Maya docs as I recall them; I could not test them in Maya here, so run it on a spare rig first.</p></div>

<div class="head blk kn" data-id="sunday-week"><div class="kicker">Your week ahead · 2 to 8 November</div><h3>Apple, an Australian rate call, French streets and a US jobs report</h3>
<template class="more"><h5>Background</h5><p>The week mixes tonight's title race, a heavy US calendar and two central bank decisions. Times are Amsterdam time. Event dates come from calendar sites (Thrive in Markets, SimianX) and the daily sections; confirm times at the source before acting.</p><h5>Key facts</h5><ul><li>Rate decisions: Reserve Bank of Australia on Tuesday 3 November (one calendar prices roughly a 70% chance of a hike to 4.85%); Bank of England on Thursday 5 November (a hike to 4.00% described as near-certain).</li><li>US October jobs report Friday 6 November, 14:30 Amsterdam time, forecast +65,000 against +29,000 in September.</li><li>Next: US CPI Tuesday 10 November (14:30), Nvidia results Tuesday 17 November (about 22:20), FOMC minutes Wednesday 18 November (20:00); next Fed decision 9 December.</li></ul><h5>What happens next</h5><p>The sharpest test for your ETFs is Friday's jobs number on top of the Nasdaq's 2.9% drop on 30 October. The sharpest test for Anthropic is an amended S-1 with a price range.</p><div class="srcs"><a href="https://thriveinmarkets.com/calendar/2026-11-02/">Thrive in Markets: week of 2 November</a> <a href="https://www.simianx.ai/stories/us-economic-calendar-this-month-in-your-time-zone">SimianX: November 2026 US market calendar</a></div></template></div>
<figure class="photo blk" data-wiki="en|Autódromo_Hermanos_Rodríguez" data-alt="Autodromo Hermanos Rodriguez"><figcaption><b>File photo.</b> The Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez in Mexico City, 2,240 m above sea level, tonight's race venue.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="box blk"><h4>Seven days, in order</h4><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Why you care</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sun 1 Nov, 20:00</td><td>Mexico City GP</td><td>Antonelli can clinch; Ferrari SF-26 on front row</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mon 2 Nov, after US close</td><td>Apple and Palantir results</td><td>Both inside the Nasdaq 100 (EQQB, 30.6% of your ETFs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tue 3 Nov</td><td>US midterms; RBA decision; AMD results</td><td>Australia mortgage and AUD angle; chip sentiment after Intel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wed 4 Nov</td><td>Kimi K3.1 Pro open weights; Uber results</td><td>Open-weight AI race, see Tech</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thu 5 Nov</td><td>France general mobilisation; Bank of England</td><td>Next test after 320,000 marchers on 31 Oct</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fri 6 Nov, 14:30</td><td>US jobs report</td><td>Moves rate-cut odds for every ETF you hold</td></tr></table></div></div>
<p class="blk">Your money. The eToro account is 15,666 dollars with +2,154 unrealised, 73.6% in one copied trader (JodySlaney, stop-loss 50%) and 26.2% in Aukie2008 (stop-loss 20%). Both are concentrated in whatever those traders hold, so a bad Apple or AMD print is a reason to look at their positions on Tuesday, not to act on Monday night. The two direct holdings are tiny: Solana 29 dollars (-6.5%) and ASML 9 dollars (-10.5%).</p>
<p class="blk">Revolut plan. Total 2,780 euros, +7.1%. Rebalance by adding money only. The most underweight lines are SEC0 (semiconductors, 6.7% against 11%) and CEBL (EM Asia, 11.0% against 15%). After Intel's crash, SEC0 is the cheapest it has been to add to, but it fell 9.0% to date for a reason: put new money in on a schedule, not in one hit before Friday's jobs report.</p>
<p class="blk">Rigging and work. Try the spline-IK masterclass above on a spare spine this week. I did not research open job-board deadlines for this issue, so check your saved alerts for closing dates.</p>
